<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="thiosulfate disproportionation IV (rhodanese)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5350" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The inorganic sulfur compound |FRAME: S2O3| contains two sulfur atoms: a sulfone-sulfur (oxidation state +V), and a sulfane-sulfur (oxidation state -I). At low pH thiosulfate decomposes spontaneously to |FRAME: SO3| and elemental sulfur |CITS: [4552952]|. At neutral pH the compound is relatively stable, but several types of bacteria can catalyze its disproportionation in a process in which thiosulfate serves as both an electron donor and an electron acceptor. Several types of enzymes have been described that are able to catalyze the disproportionation of thiosulfate. These enzymes have been differentiated based on the nature of the electron donor: some enzymes require thiols for this purpose, some utilize cytochrome c3, which receives the electrons from molecular hydrogen via a hydrogenase, some accept the electrons from the quinone pool, and some have only been active &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; when coupled to cyanide. These four classes of enzymes are described in the pathways |FRAME:PWY-5277|, |FRAME:PWY-5352|, |FRAME: PWY-7813|, and |FRAME:PWY-5350|, respectively. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Thiosulfate sulfurtransferase is more often referred to by the name rhodanese, from the German word for thiocyanate, &quot;rhodanid&quot;. The enzyme catalyzes the transfer of a sulfur atom from suitable sulfur donors to nucleophilic sulfur acceptors. The original description of rhodanese, purified from bovine mitochondria, used thiosulfate and cyanide for this purpose. Rhodanese is a widespread enzyme, and has been detected in many major phyla, both prokaryotic and eukaryotic |CITS: [6357923]|. Despite its ubiquity, the physiological role of rhodanese has not yet been established unambiguously. It has been suggested that rhodanese is involved in detoxification of cyanide in both mammals |CITS: [3073057][10762072]| and bacteria |CITS: [18685272]|. It has also been proposed that rhodanese, using the dithiol |FRAME:CPD-296| as the sulfur acceptor, may act as a sulfur insertase involved in the formation of iron-sulfur clusters in proteins such as ferredoxin |CITS: [6430704][2983992]|. Rhodanese performs the reaction by a double displacement formal mechanism. The crystal structure of rhodanese from |FRAME: TAX-354| has been determined at 1.8 &amp;Aring;, and the study revealed that the active form of the enzyme contains a persulfide, where a sulfur is attached to the active cysteine residue |CITS: [10788330]|. Monothiols such as glutathione have been reported to be poor substrates for rhodanese |CITS: [14086740][5333153][10735872]|. However, more recent work has suggested that at least some rhodanese enzymes utilize glutathione as their major sulfur receipient |CITS: [37237993]|. The distinction between thiosulfate reductase and rhodanese is not always straight forward. For example, Aird et al purified an enzyme from |FRAME: TAX-471| that could catalyze either reaction under different conditions |CITS: [3480285]|.</Description>
